[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 764,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 701,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 120732,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 194,
        "headingId": 701,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Alpha heavy chain disease (HCD) is a variant of extranodal marginal zone lymphoma of mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (EMZL) that typically involves the gastrointestinal tract and secretes defective, truncated alpha heavy chains lacking light chains.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T12:03:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 765,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 702,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 120733,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 194,
        "headingId": 702,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>9762/3 Alpha heavy chain disease</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T12:03:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 766,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 703,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 120734,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 194,
        "headingId": 703,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>2A84.0 Alpha heavy chain disease</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T12:03:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 767,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 704,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 120735,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 194,
        "headingId": 704,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> Mediterranean lymphoma; immunoproliferative small intestinal disease (IPSID).</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T12:03:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 768,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 705,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 120736,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 194,
        "headingId": 705,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T12:03:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 769,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 706,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 120737,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 194,
        "headingId": 706,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>The duodenum is most often involved (63% of cases), followed by the jejunum (17%) and ileum (8%). Involvement of the stomach, colon, mesenteric lymph nodes, respiratory tract, thyroid, or more than one anatomical site has been reported in rare cases {21276391; 29372346}.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T12:03:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 770,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 707,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 120738,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 194,
        "headingId": 707,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>The most common gastrointestinal manifestations are malabsorption with diarrhoea, abdominal pain, weight loss, hypoalbuminaemia, and electrolyte abnormalities {21276391; 29372346; 34270561}.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T12:03:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 771,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 708,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 120739,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 194,
        "headingId": 708,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Patients are predominantly young and male, and the incidence is higher in the Mediterranean, Middle East, and low- and middle-income countries than in other geographical areas {34270561}. The overall incidence is declining following broader accessibility of antibiotics.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T12:03:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 772,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 709,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 120740,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 194,
        "headingId": 709,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Impaired localized immunity secondary to prior infection leads to diminished clearance of <em>Campylobacter jejuni</em>. Chronic <em>C.&nbsp;jejuni</em> infection and toxin exposure induce an IgA-secreting lymphoplasmacytic proliferation that acquires somatic hypermutation and double-stranded DNA breaks, ultimately resulting in clonal expansion {29372346}.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T12:03:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 773,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 710,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 120741,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 194,
        "headingId": 710,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Alpha HCD arises from a clonal expansion of cells producing abnormal truncated alpha heavy chains with loss of light chain binding sites and B-cell receptor idiotype, allowing immune escape. Mutant heavy chains cluster with the B-cell receptor, resulting in activated signal transduction, antigen-independent proliferation, and tumour cell growth {29372346}.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T12:03:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 780,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 717,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 120742,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 194,
        "headingId": 717,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Endoscopy shows polypoid or nodular lesions, erythematous mucosal thickening, or mucosal oedema {29372346; 34270561}.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T12:03:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 774,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 711,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 120743,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 194,
        "headingId": 711,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Tissue biopsies contain a dense infiltrate composed of abundant plasma or lymphoplasmacytic cells with splaying of intestinal crypts and villous atrophy. Other features of EMZL may also be present (see <em>Histology</em> in <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/63/127\" target=\"_blank\">section </a><em>Extranodal marginal zone lymphoma of mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue</em>). Large-cell transformation with sheets of immunoblasts or plasmablasts may occur.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T12:03:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 775,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 712,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 120744,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 194,
        "headingId": 712,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>EUS-FNAB may be used to augment an endoscopic mucosal biopsy and collect material for ancillary testing.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T12:03:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 776,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 713,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 120745,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 194,
        "headingId": 713,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Clonal IG gene rearrangements are present. No known recurrent mutations or translocations are known, including, for example, rearrangements involving <em>MALT1</em> or other typical aberrations found in EMZL {29372346}.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T12:03:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 779,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 716,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 120746,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 194,
        "headingId": 716,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> tissue biopsy with features of EMZL with extensive plasmacytic differentiation expressing IgA without light chains.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> serum immunofixation demonstrating anti-alpha reactivity without associated light chain.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T12:03:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 777,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 714,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 120747,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 194,
        "headingId": 714,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>The disease is staged as localized (single-organ involvement) or systemic (nodal and multiorgan involvement) {29372346}.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T12:03:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 778,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 715,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 120748,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 194,
        "headingId": 715,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>The natural history is local progression followed by systemic spread. Complications of bowel obstruction or perforation, severe malnutrition, or infection may be fatal. In early disease, 6-month antimicrobial treatment leads to remission in 30&ndash;70% of cases, although recurrence is common {29372346}. Anthracycline-based chemotherapy and rituximab have been effective in treating systemic disease or large-cell transformation {34270561}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T12:03:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]